  "problem": "![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/capture/linear/14155061/assets/a583013eef64a37770bae73f1b17d0df310e1e290e44f1f8a500ccd543346d51.png)\n\nUse the model above to determine the missing number in the equation below.\n\n<table align=\"center\" cellpadding=\"1\" rules=\"all\">\n\n<tbody><tr> \n<td width=\"25\" height=\"25\" align=\"center\">$3$</td>\n<td rowspan=\"2\" width=\"25\" align=\"center\">$÷$</td>\n<td width=\"25\" height=\"25\" align=\"center\">$1$</td>\n<td rowspan=\"2\" width=\"25\" align=\"center\">$=$</td>\n<td width=\"25\" height=\"25\" align=\"center\">\n{{field-1}}\n</td>\n</tr>\n\n<tr> \n<td width=\"25\" height=\"25\" align=\"center\">$4$</td>\n<td width=\"25\" height=\"25\" align=\"center\">$2$</td>\n<td width=\"25\" height=\"25\" align=\"center\">$2$</td>\n</tr>\n\n</tbody></table>",
  "worked_solution": "The denominators are $4$ and $2.$ We can make a common denominator of $4$ by splitting the shape on the right into $2$ parts vertically:\n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/capture/linear/14155061/assets/c401472d271b22b1461c6f02888196a29b6b5b1d069d52638c325e807caab8c0.png)\n\nSo, our division problem now is\n\n$$\n\\frac{3}{4}÷\\frac{2}{4}.\n$$\n\nSince the denominators are equal, this is equivalent to\n\n$$\n3÷2=\\frac{3}{2}.\n$$\n\nTherefore, we conclude that\n\n$$\n\\frac{3}{4}÷\\frac{1}{2}=\\frac{3}{2}\\,.\n$$",
